---
/**
 * website/src/pages/console.astro — the console page (website plan, Task W1.6; the owner's
 * approved board `website/design-ref/ConsoleV2.dc.html`, 2026-10-20).
 *
 * The spec's section 4 asks for *"Console downloads: per-OS download links"*, and the console
 * has shipped: `console/` holds the application and the workflow that signs, notarises and
 * staples its macOS artifacts, that workflow created the `console-v0.9.0` release, and
 * `features.console_downloads` is true. So this page is the console's own page — what it is,
 * what it looks like, what a reader downloads and the three properties its own decisions fix
 * — and the placeholder the section renders while the flag is off is still here, one build
 * away, because a copy of this repository is built into it (`website/test/console.mjs`).
 *
 * **The page is the board's four blocks, in the board's order**: the hero (the release's own
 * tag and platform, the name, what the console is and the two actions), the screenshot slider
 * (`ScreenshotSlider.astro`), the downloads (`Downloads.astro`) and the facts. Each block is
 * a component or a section of this file; nothing is drawn twice.
 *
 * Where its facts come from, and why nothing here is typed into the markup:
 *
 *   - `website/content/downloads.json` — the console's own facts, read and validated by
 *     `src/lib/console-downloads.mjs`: what the console is, the sentence about the release,
 *     the properties the console's own decisions fix (read-only, no telemetry, its own
 *     artifact), the release its download URLs are built from, and one download per published
 *     asset. Every sentence below is rendered from that file, and `website/test/console.mjs`
 *     asserts that: a page that writes its own copy of a fact is the copy that drifts first
 *     (F37, Global Constraint 6).
 *   - `website/data/site.yaml` — the site's own facts, read through `loadSite()`: the
 *     feature flag `features.console_downloads`, which is the flag the Go boundary test
 *     holds the whole of `website/` to (`internal/cli/docs_website_boundary_test.go`,
 *     `TestWebsiteLinksNoConsoleWhileTheFlagIsOff`), and the `repository` every download
 *     URL is composed from.
 *   - `website/data/console-screens.yaml` — the console's screenshots, read through
 *     `loadConsoleScreens()`: the four fixture captures the owner asked for on 2026-10-21
 *     ("console page: screenshots missing"), served from `public/screenshots/`. The slider
 *     renders the rows it is handed, and a list with no row in it draws no slider at all.
 *
 * **The headline is the data file's name, split and not rewritten.** The board draws the
 * console's name in two lines with the last two words in the accent's gradient, so the line is
 * split here the way the home page's headline is: the split is computed from the name itself,
 * which is what keeps the rendered `h1` the data file's string rather than a second copy of it
 * with a `<span>` in the middle (`website/src/components/Hero.astro`, D102).
 *
 * **The two actions are the page's own two phrases, and each one is held to the data file.**
 * "Download for macOS" names the platform of the release's first published asset, and "Install
 * the CLI first" is a clause of the data file's own sentence about the release — so the guard
 * below refuses a build whose data file has stopped saying either. The first one is rendered
 * only while the console has shipped: a page that offered a download for a console with no
 * release would be pointing a reader at the section that says there is none.
 *
 * **The reader is shared, because two pages offer the console.** Getting Started's console
 * option renders the same artifact from the same module (the owner's changes spec of
 * 2026-10-18, item 6), so `src/lib/console-downloads.mjs` holds the two flags' rule, the
 * release's own shape and the URL derivation, and this page only renders what it returns.
 *
 * **The two flags are one fact, and the build refuses them disagreeing.** `site.yaml`'s
 * flag is what the repository-wide rule reads; `downloads.json`'s `available` is what the
 * sections render from. A build that rendered one state while the other said the opposite
 * is exactly the drift the placeholder exists to prevent, so the disagreement stops the
 * build, naming both files — the same shape `content.ts` gives the analytics flag and its
 * measurement id (W1.3 step 2).
 *
 * **And a URL may not exist under `website/` at all.** Every download URL is composed at
 * build time from the release's published names, so no file carries one — the state the Go
 * boundary test scans the whole site for while the flag is false (Global Constraint 9).
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import Downloads from '../components/Downloads.astro'
import ScreenshotSlider from '../components/ScreenshotSlider.astro'
import { SITE_ROOT, loadConsoleScreens, loadSite } from '../content.ts'
import { loadConsoleDownloads } from '../lib/console-downloads.mjs'

// The whole generated layer is checked first — every page renders through the base layout,
// and this call is what a page does before it reads a fact of its own (W1.3 step 2).
const site = loadSite()
const facts = loadConsoleDownloads(SITE_ROOT, site.facts.features.consoleDownloads, site.facts.repository)

// The screenshots, above the downloads (owner feedback 2026-10-19; the four captures landed on
// 2026-10-21). The slider renders the data file's rows — the pictures, their alt text and their
// captions — and a data file with no row in it renders no slider section at all.
const { screens } = loadConsoleScreens()

/**
 * The two halves of the headline: the words before the gradient, and the words in it. The
 * board draws `The Fugaro` in the text tone and `desktop console` in the accent's gradient,
 * and **the split is taken from the name** rather than written here, so the `h1` a reader
 * receives is still one string of the data file's with markup around its last words. Two
 * words is the board's split, and a name of two words or fewer is a defect in this file
 * rather than a headline with an empty first half.
 */
const GRADIENT_WORDS = 2
const nameWords = facts.name.split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean)
if (nameWords.length <= GRADIENT_WORDS) {
  throw new Error(
    `website/src/pages/console.astro: ${JSON.stringify(facts.name)} is ${nameWords.length} word(s), and the hero draws its last ${GRADIENT_WORDS} in the accent's gradient: a headline with nothing before the gradient is the split this page was written for, and the data file's name moved.`,
  )
}
const HEADLINE_HEAD = nameWords.slice(0, -GRADIENT_WORDS).join(' ')
const HEADLINE_GRADIENT = nameWords.slice(-GRADIENT_WORDS).join(' ')

/**
 * The status pill above the headline: the release a reader is looking at and the platform it
 * is built for, both read out of the data file (`release.tag` is the prefix and the version
 * composed, and the platform is the first published asset's own). The board draws the two
 * together with a dot between them, and what makes this the repository's rather than this
 * page's is the guard below: the platform is held to the data file's sentence about the
 * release, so a pill can never name one no document speaks about.
 *
 * **The platform is `null` unless there is a release to read one from**, and that is what the
 * pill and the download action are drawn on — not on `available` itself, and never on a bare
 * first element of the list. Two states are the reason, and neither is hypothetical:
 *
 *   - an unavailable data file may carry the artifacts it will publish — the loader permits
 *     `available: false` with a list of downloads, and the section renders none of them — so a
 *     file that has not shipped can name a platform, and a hero that read the list would make
 *     a claim about a release this repository says does not exist;
 *   - and the opposite pair (`available: true` with an empty list) is refused by the loader,
 *     naming the file (`src/lib/console-downloads.mjs`, "available is true and downloads is
 *     empty"), which is why the read below asks for a first element rather than taking one:
 *     the page's own guard against that state is its silence, because a `TypeError` from an
 *     array position is a build with no message in it.
 */
const RELEASE_TAG = facts.release.tag
const RELEASE_PLATFORM: string | null =
  facts.available && facts.downloads.length > 0 ? facts.downloads[0].platform : null

/**
 * The two actions. The first names the platform the release's first asset is built for — the
 * same platform the pill above carries, so the page's one download claim is made once and
 * followed by the reader in one place — and the second is the data file's own clause about
 * where a reader starts. Both are held to the data file below, and the first is `null` when
 * there is no release to download.
 */
const CTA_DOWNLOAD: string | null = RELEASE_PLATFORM === null ? null : `Download for ${RELEASE_PLATFORM}`
const CTA_CLI = 'Install the CLI first'

// Hold the two actions and the pill to the data file's own sentence about the release. Each
// name is a fact this page renders rather than a fact it holds: a platform the release's
// sentence does not carry, or a "CLI first" the sentence has stopped saying, is this page
// saying something no document of this repository says (Global Constraint 6) — and the day the
// release's sentence is reworded, this is what stops the build rather than shipping a claim
// with nothing behind it. A phrase that is `null` is a hero that draws nothing for it, so
// there is nothing to hold.
for (const phrase of [RELEASE_PLATFORM, CTA_CLI]) {
  if (phrase === null) continue
  if (!facts.released.text.includes(phrase)) {
    throw new Error(
      `website/src/pages/console.astro: the hero names ${JSON.stringify(phrase)} and ${JSON.stringify(facts.released.text)} — the data file's own sentence about the release — does not carry it: the hero's pill, its download action and its CLI link are written out of that sentence, and a page that names a fact its own data file does not is the page contradicting its data (Global Constraint 6).`,
    )
  }
}
---

<Base title="Console" description={facts.what.text}>
  <article>
    {/* The board's first screen: what the console is, and the two things a reader does
        next. It is a section with a name rather than a bare `div`, so the landmark is one a
        screen reader can list, and it is the only block on the page with the board's
        background glow behind it. */}
    <section class="console-hero" aria-labelledby="console-title">
      <div class="hero-glow" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <div class="console-hero-copy">
        {/* The release, while the repository says it has shipped: the tag its assets are
            published under and the platform they are built for, both the data file's. */}
        {RELEASE_PLATFORM !== null && (
          <p class="pill console-pill">
            <span class="console-pill-dot" aria-hidden="true" />
            {RELEASE_TAG} · {RELEASE_PLATFORM}
          </p>
        )}

        <h1 class="console-title display-xl" id="console-title">
          {HEADLINE_HEAD}{' '}
          <span class="gradient-text">{HEADLINE_GRADIENT}</span>
        </h1>

        {/* What the console is, in the data file's own sentence: a live view of your runs,
            with no backend and no credential of its own. */}
        <p class="console-lead">{facts.what.text}</p>

        {/* What a reader does next: take the release, or read how to start with the CLI.
            The first is rendered only while there is a release to take, and both are the
            page's own words for actions the data file's sentence describes. */}
        <p class="console-actions">
          {CTA_DOWNLOAD !== null && (
            <a class="console-cta" href="#downloads">
              {CTA_DOWNLOAD}
            </a>
          )}
          <a class="console-cta-quiet" href="/getting-started/">
            {CTA_CLI}
          </a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* What the console looks like, before what it costs to get it: the slider renders the
        data file's rows — the four fixture captures — and a data file with no row in it
        renders no slider at all. */}
    <ScreenshotSlider screens={screens} />

    {/* The downloads themselves: one card per platform, rendered from `downloads.json`, and
        rendering no link and no button at all while the console is unreleased (W1.6 step 1). */}
    <Downloads
      available={facts.available}
      note={facts.notReleased.text}
      released={facts.released}
      downloads={facts.downloads}
      linux={facts.linux}
    />

    {/* The three properties the console's own decisions fix, in the board's cards: what it
        does with a reader's cluster (nothing), what it sends (nothing) and where it comes
        from (its own release). Each card is the data file's own title and sentence. */}
    <section id="console-facts" aria-labelledby="console-facts-heading">
      <h2 id="console-facts-heading" class="display-lg">Facts about the console</h2>
      <ul class="console-facts">
        {
          facts.properties.map((property) => (
            <li class="card console-fact">
              <h3 class="console-fact-title">{property.title}</h3>
              <p class="console-fact-text">{property.text}</p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Tokens only (W1.2): every colour below is the palette in force, so the contrast check in
     `website/test/a11y.mjs` is about the colours this page actually paints. */

  /* The hero: the board's centred first screen, with the site's own glow behind it
     (`base.css`'s `.hero-glow`, which needs a positioned, clipped parent). */
  .console-hero {
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2xl);
  }

  .console-hero-copy {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-md);
    padding: var(--space-2xl) 0 var(--space-xl);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .console-pill {
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
  }

  /* The pill's dot: the success tone, which is the one colour on the site that means "this is
     running" — the same mark the home page's pill carries. A mark and not text, so it carries
     no pair of its own. */
  .console-pill-dot {
    flex: none;
    width: 8px;
    height: 8px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    background: var(--ok);
  }

  /* The headline: `base.css`'s `.display-xl`, the display scale's largest step, with the one
     thing that is this page's own — a measure that keeps the name to the two lines the board
     sets it in rather than running the width of a wide window. */
  .console-title {
    max-width: 20ch;
  }

  /* The lead under it: the data file's sentence about what the console is, at the size the
     board sets a lead and no wider than a comfortable measure. */
  .console-lead {
    max-width: 60ch;
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--font-size-xl);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
  }

  /* The two actions, side by side and wrapping to two rows on a phone. */
  .console-actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: var(--space-md);
    margin: var(--space-xs) 0 0;
  }

  /* The filled action: the accent as the fill and the ground as the label, which is the one
     colour on the page that means "this is the action" — the same shape the home page's call
     to action is drawn in. It is written here rather than in `base.css` so that this page's
     run touches no shared file; a second page needing it is the day it moves there. */
  .console-cta {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    min-height: 44px;
    padding: var(--space-sm) var(--space-lg);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    background: var(--accent);
    color: var(--ground);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
    font-weight: var(--weight-black);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  /* The second action is a link, not a second button: it keeps the underline `base.css` gives
     every link in running text, so it reads as a link rather than as a disabled control beside
     the filled one — and it is a control all the same, so it carries the same 44 px minimum. */
  .console-cta-quiet {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    min-height: 44px;
    font-size: var(--font-size-base);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
  }

  /* The facts: the board's three cards, in the site's own grid shape, so a fourth property in
     the data file needs no rule of its own. */
  .console-facts {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(18rem, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-md);
    margin: var(--space-lg) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .console-fact {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
  }

  .console-fact-title {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--font-size-xl);
    font-weight: var(--weight-black);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-tight);
  }

  .console-fact-text {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
